How can I create a context-free grammar in C++ that includes a symbol or rule with a nested, recursive definition? Can you provide an example of how this grammar can be used to generate valid strings in the language it describes?
Here is an example of a context-free grammar with a nested, recursive rule:
S → aSb | ε
This grammar generates strings of the form a^n b^n where n >= 0. The rule S → ε allows for the possibility of an empty string.
Here is the C++ code for implementing this grammar:
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;
void generate_strings(string str, int a_count, int b_count)
{
    if (a_count == 0 && b_count == 0) {
        cout << str << endl;
        return;
    }

    if (a_count > 0) {
        generate_strings(str + "a", a_count - 1, b_count);
    }

    if (b_count > a_count) {
        generate_strings(str + "b", a_count, b_count - 1);
    }
}
int main()
{
    int n;
    cout << "Enter the value of n: ";
    cin >> n;

    generate_strings("", n, n);

    return 0;
}
This code prompts the user to enter a value for n, then generates all possible strings of the form a^n b^n using the grammar. The generate_strings() function uses recursion to build up the string, adding an "a" or "b" to the end of the current string until the desired number of "a"s and "b"s have been added. Finally, it outputs the generated string.

