```
As for incorporating the conservation of energy principle into C++ code, we can do this by keeping track of the total energy in a system and making sure it doesn't change over time. Here's an example of how this could be done using the kinetic energy formula, potential energy formula, and work formula:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <cmath>
using namespace std;
double calculateKineticEnergy(double mass, double velocity) {
    return 0.5 * mass * pow(velocity, 2);
}
double calculatePotentialEnergy(double mass, double height) {
    double gravitationalPotentialEnergy = mass * 9.81 * height;
    return gravitationalPotentialEnergy;
}
double calculateWork(double force, double distance) {
    return force * distance;
}
int main() {
    double mass = 10; // in kg
    double velocity = 5; // in m/s
    double height = 20; // in m
    double force = 50; // in N
    double distance = 10; // in m
    double initialTotalEnergy = calculateKineticEnergy(mass, velocity) + calculatePotentialEnergy(mass, height);
    cout << "Initial total energy: " << initialTotalEnergy << " joules." << endl;
    double workDone = calculateWork(force, distance);
    double finalTotalEnergy = initialTotalEnergy - workDone;
    cout << "Work done: " << workDone << " joules." << endl;
    cout << "Final total energy: " << finalTotalEnergy << " joules." << endl;
    if (abs(finalTotalEnergy - initialTotalEnergy) < 0.0001) {
        cout << "Energy is conserved!" << endl;
    } else {
        cout << "Energy is not conserved." << endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
```
In this example, we calculate the initial total energy of a system using the kinetic energy formula and potential energy formula. We then calculate the work done on the system using the work formula, and subtract that from the initial total energy to get the final total energy. Finally, we check if the difference between the initial and final total energy is within a small threshold (0.0001 joules in this case) to ensure that energy is conserved.

