Sure, here's a JavaScript function that can generate a datamap based on the input sentence:
```
function generateDatamap(sentence) {
  const datamap = {
    intention: '',
    action: '',
    items: [],
    foundItems: false
  };

  const words = sentence.split(' ');

  for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
    const word = words[i].toLowerCase();

    if (word === 'intending' || word === 'intended') {
      datamap.intention = words[i + 2];
    }

    if (word === 'went' || word === 'go') {
      datamap.action = words[i + 2];
    }

    if (word === 'to') {
      const nextWord = words[i + 1].toLowerCase();
      if (nextWord === 'buy' || nextWord === 'purchase') {
        datamap.items.push(words[i + 2]);
      }
    }

    if (word === 'but' && words[i + 1] === 'could' && words[i + 2] === 'not' && words[i + 3] === 'find') {
      datamap.foundItems = true;
    }
  }

  return datamap;
}
```
This function takes in a sentence as a parameter and returns a datamap object containing the relevant information from the sentence. The datamap has four properties:
- `intention`: the item the person intended to purchase
- `action`: the action the person took (e.g. "went to the store")
- `items`: an array of the items the person intended to buy
- `foundItems`: a boolean indicating whether or not the person was able to find the items they wanted to buy
The function works by splitting the sentence into an array of words and then iterating through each word to find the relevant information. It checks for words like "intending," "went," "to buy," and "could not find" to determine the values for each property.
