Utilizing ground robots for exploring hazardous environments has several advantages. Firstly, it minimizes the risk of human casualties as the robots can be sent to areas where it is unsafe for humans to go. Secondly, ground robots can operate in areas with extreme temperatures, radiation, or toxic gas levels, which would be impossible for humans to withstand. Finally, robots can work for extended periods without rest or food, making them ideal for long-term missions.
In terms of programming ground robots to operate in hazardous environments using MATLAB, some of the ways include:
1. Path Planning: Ground robots can be programmed to navigate through hazardous environments by creating a path planning algorithm. The algorithm takes into account the obstacles in the environment and generates a safe path for the robot to follow. Here is an example code for path planning in MATLAB:
```matlab
map = binaryOccupancyMap(10,10,10);
inflate(map,0.5);
start = [2 2];
goal = [8 8];
planner = robotics.PRM;
planner.Map = map;
planner.NumNodes = 50;
planner.ConnectionDistance = 1;
path = findpath(planner,start,goal)
```
2. Obstacle Avoidance: Ground robots can be programmed to detect obstacles in their path and avoid them. This is done using sensors such as LIDAR or ultrasonic sensors. Here is an example code for obstacle avoidance in MATLAB:
```matlab
robot = robotics.RoboticsSystem('Name', 'ObstacleAvoidance');
sensor = robotics.UltrasonicSensor('Name', 'UltrasonicSensor');
sensor.MountDirection = 'back';
robot.addSensor(sensor);
% Read sensor data
