Yes, I can help you with that. Here's an example implementation of the `detect_sentence_type` function using NLP techniques in MATLAB:
```
function [sentenceType] = detect_sentence_type(sentence)
    % Load the Stanford CoreNLP Java library
    javaaddpath('/path/to/stanford-corenlp-full-2018-10-05/stanford-corenlp-3.9.2.jar');
    javaaddpath('/path/to/stanford-corenlp-full-2018-10-05/stanford-corenlp-3.9.2-models.jar');

    % Create a CoreNLP pipeline
    props = java.util.Properties();
    props.setProperty('annotators', 'tokenize,ssplit,pos');
    pipeline = edu.stanford.nlp.pipeline.StanfordCoreNLP(props);

    % Process the sentence with CoreNLP
    document = edu.stanford.nlp.pipeline.CoreDocument(sentence);
    pipeline.annotate(document);

    % Get the sentence tokens and POS tags
    tokens = document.tokens();
    posTags = cellfun(@(x) char(x.tag()), tokens.toArray(), 'UniformOutput', false);

    % Check if the sentence is an imperative or declarative sentence
    if strcmp(posTags{end}, '.') && strcmp(posTags{1}, 'VB')
        sentenceType = 'imperative';
    else
        sentenceType = 'declarative';
    end
end
```
In this implementation, we use the Stanford CoreNLP Java library to tokenize the sentence and identify its POS tags. We then check if the last token is a period and the first token is a verb. If so, we classify the sentence as an imperative sentence. Otherwise, we classify it as a declarative sentence.
