To reverse a linked list in Java, follow these steps:

1. Create a temporary node (prev) and initialize it as null.

2. Create a current node (current) and initialize it to the head of the linked list.

3. Loop through the linked list by traversing through the nodes, until current node is null.

4. In each iteration of the loop, set the next pointer of current to the previous node (prev).

5. Update the previous node (prev) to the current node (which will be the next node in the original list).

6. Update the current node (current) to the next node (which will be the next node in the original list).

7. When the loop is complete, set the head of the linked list to the last node (which is now the first node of the reversed list).

8. Return the head of the reversed list.

Here's the code for reversing a linked list:

```
public ListNode reverseList(ListNode head) {
   ListNode prev = null;
   ListNode current = head;
   while (current != null) {
       ListNode next = current.next;
       current.next = prev;
       prev = current;
       current = next;
   }
   head = prev;
   return head;
}
```

Note: The ListNode class represents a single node in the linked list, and has two fields: val (which represents the value of the node), and next (which represents the next node in the list).

Yes, I can develop a C++ program that implements the Quickselect algorithm to determine the median of an unsorted array of integers. Here is the code:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;
int partition(int arr[], int left, int right, int pivotIndex) {
    int pivotValue = arr[pivotIndex];
    swap(arr[pivotIndex], arr[right]);
    int storeIndex = left;
    for (int i = left; i < right; i++) {
        if (arr[i] < pivotValue) {
            swap(arr[i], arr[storeIndex]);
            storeIndex++;
        }
    }
    swap(arr[storeIndex], arr[right]);
    return storeIndex;
}
int quickselect(int arr[], int left, int right, int k) {
    if (left == right) {
        return arr[left];
    }
    int pivotIndex = left + rand() % (right - left + 1);
    pivotIndex = partition(arr, left, right, pivotIndex);
    if (k == pivotIndex) {
        return arr[k];
    } else if (k < pivotIndex) {
        return quickselect(arr, left, pivotIndex - 1, k);
    } else {
        return quickselect(arr, pivotIndex + 1, right, k);
    }
}
int median(int arr[], int size) {
    if (size % 2 == 0) {
        return (quickselect(arr, 0, size - 1, size / 2 - 1) + quickselect(arr, 0, size - 1, size / 2)) / 2;
    } else {
        return quickselect(arr, 0, size - 1, size / 2);
    }
}
int main() {
    int arr[] = {3, 5, 2, 6, 1, 4};
    int size = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);
    cout << "The median of the array is: " << median(arr, size) << endl;
    return 0;
}
```
The Quickselect algorithm is a selection algorithm that can find the k-th smallest element in an unsorted array in O(n) time on average. The algorithm works by selecting a pivot element from the array and partitioning the other elements into two sub-arrays, according to whether they are less than or greater than the pivot. The pivot element is then compared with the desired k-th element, and the algorithm recurses on the appropriate sub-array until the pivot is the desired element.
In this implementation, the `partition` function takes an array, a left index, a right index, and a pivot index as input, and partitions the array into two sub-arrays by swapping elements. The `quickselect` function takes an array, a left index, a right index, and a k-th element as input, and recursively partitions the array until the k-th element is found. Finally, the `median` function takes an array and its size as input, and returns the median value by calling the `quickselect` function with the appropriate k-th element.
To optimize the program for an average-case time complexity of O(n), we randomly select the pivot element using `rand() % (right - left + 1)` in the `quickselect` function. This ensures that the pivot element is chosen randomly and reduces the likelihood of selecting a bad pivot that would result in a worst-case time complexity of O(n^2).

