```
If we apply the linear search algorithm to this problem, it will take up to 10 iterations to find the index of the integer 7. However, if we apply the binary search algorithm to the same problem, it will take only 3 iterations to find the index of the integer 7.
Runtime Complexity:
The runtime complexity of linear search is O(n), where n is the size of the array. This means that the time it takes to execute the algorithm increases linearly with the size of the input.
The runtime complexity of binary search is O(log n), where n is the size of the array. This means that the time it takes to execute the algorithm increases logarithmically with the size of the input.
Suitability:
Linear search is suitable for small arrays or lists where the elements are not sorted. Binary search is suitable for larger arrays or lists where the elements are sorted.
In conclusion, if you have a small array or list that is not sorted, linear search is a suitable algorithm to use. However, if you have a larger array or list that is sorted, binary search is a more efficient algorithm to use.

